## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a production-grade control plane feature that enforces resource fairness, minimizes disruption, and scales across tenants and clusters.

## Key Concepts
- CRD-driven budgets per node and per-namespace priorities
- Reconciliation loop with breach detection, drain/re-schedule, and preemption safety
- Interaction with PodDisruptionBudget, CSI volumes, and QoS tiers
- Observability: metrics, alerts, audit trails, and tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler skeleton for NodeBudget controller
class NodeBudgetController {
  reconcile(nodeBudgetCR) {
    const node = getNode(nodeBudgetCR.spec.nodeName)
    const budget = nodeBudgetCR.spec.budget
    const usage = getNodeUsage(node)
    if (usage.cpu > budget.cpu || usage.mem > budget.mem) {
      const candidates = selectNonCriticalPods(node)
      drainPods(candidates)
      rescheduleToHealthyNodes(candidates)
      emitEventsAndMetrics(node, usage, budget)
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model priority and fairness when multiple nodes breach budgets simultaneously?
- How would you test this with a simulated workload and ensure no data loss during drains?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of namespace boundaries, ResourceQuota, and how quotas constrain deployments in real pipelines. Candidates must reason about requests/limits, replica sizing, and CI gatekeeping.

## Key Concepts

- ResourceQuota and per-namespace limits
- Resource requests vs. limits
- kubectl describe/get quota; observing quota enforcement
- CI gating and pre-deploy checks for quota headroom

